ippfix (1.0.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reports now cross-check the printer's job accounting against its own RFC
    3805 page counter over SNMP. job-impressions-completed comes from the
    firmware that just claimed success for a job it did not print; the page
    counter comes from the marking engine. A job the printer says it printed
    while its counter did not move is a failure nothing else here would have
    noticed, and is now reported.
  * The counter is checked before it is believed -- the printer is asked what
    its counter counts -- and switched off, loudly, if it goes backwards twice,
    jumps implausibly, stops answering, or repeatedly fails to move for jobs
    reporting impressions. It never raises an alert of its own until it has
    been seen to move for a job that did print. --no-page-counter, or
    ?page-counter=off per printer.
  * New optional SNMP relay, so the printer's counters are reachable from
    networks the printer is not. Read-only GET and GETNEXT inside the Printer
    MIB; GETBULK, SET, SNMPv3 and everything outside those subtrees are
    refused; rate limited per source and overall. Its socket is shipped
    disabled: opening a UDP port is the administrator's decision.
  * The relay can serve several printers, one per address: ?snmp-relay=ADDRESS
    names the listener a printer owns, and listeners are matched to printers by
    the address they bound rather than by what the units are called. With
    several printers and only a wildcard listener it answers nothing and says
    so, rather than picking one.
  * Per-printer settings now travel as a query string on the printer's URI,
    and an unrecognised one is an error rather than silently ignored.
  * SNMP strings are stripped of the NUL padding printer firmware adds, and
    printer status strings are collapsed to one line before they reach a
    report: an HP M430 answers its console text with an embedded newline, which
    in a mailed report would forge a line of it.
  * snmpmini.py replaces the SNMP implementation that probe-printer.py carried
    privately, so the daemon and the probe read the counter the same way.

 -- gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 09:44:24 -0700

ippfix (1.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reports about a job that did not print now carry what is needed to
    reproduce it: the job as the client sent it, when --archive is on, and the
    job as this proxy handed it to the printer. --alert-max-attachment bounds
    what a report may carry.
  * Reports also record what the printer said about itself at the time. An
    empty cartridge prints nothing and blames nobody, and that is worth ruling
    out before blaming firmware.
  * DNS-SD publishes the advertised address rather than a .local name, so
    printing no longer depends on multicast DNS reaching this host every time.
    --advertise-hostname restores a name where one is wanted.
  * Alerts were queued but never delivered. sendmail(1) forks a delivery agent
    and returns, and the default KillMode killed that agent as soon as the
    oneshot unit's ExecStart exited -- mid-SMTP, silently. KillMode=process in
    ippfix-alert.service, and a drop-in for dma.service, which flushes its
    queue the same way and loses mail for the same reason.
  * StartLimitIntervalSec was in [Service], where systemd ignores it, so the
    proxy did not in fact retry indefinitely as its own comment claimed.
  * New ippfix-archive-reminder: schedules a one-shot reminder that --archive
    is still on, and deletes itself once it has run.

 -- gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 07:46:30 -0700

